@use Compiler

class SlytherScript {
    Wraps a string, represents the source code of a SlytherScript
}

class SlytherArtifact {
    Represents an instantiated artifact defined in an #{SlytherScript}, contains:

    - artifact: The kind of the artifact, ex. class, function, etc...
    - name: The name of the artifact to be created, optionally qualified as `Parent::child` to declare it
      inside another artifact; the parent must be declared too, otherwise it must throw an error
    - args: The arguments declared between parens after the name, as `name: value` pairs, in the order written.
      Each value is parsed by the way it is written: quoted is a string, digits are a number, true and false are a
      boolean, and a bare symbol is a type, which must be a built-in type or a declared artifact, otherwise it
      must throw an error. The built-in types are string, number and boolean. A type followed by `?` is optional,
      and anything else followed by `?` must throw an error
    - qualifiers: The qualifiers declared after a colon that follows the name and the args, as bare words separated
      by commas, in the order written. Writing the same qualifier twice must throw an error
    - content: The prose written inside the artifact, or the text of the file it is taken `from`, and empty when it is a `ref`
    - source: When the prose is taken from a file instead of a body, whether `from` or `ref`, the path of the file
      relative to the folder everything runs from, and the hash of what the file held. A `ref` may name a folder
      instead, kept with a trailing separator and hashed by #{SlytherFolderSource}
    - prose: The prose as whoever runs from that folder reads it: the content, or, for a ref, a line telling to read
      its file, or the files its folder holds
    - references: `kind:name` of the references found in the artifact prose and of the types of its args, defined as \#{Reference}, escaped as \\#{Reference}, if the symbol is not found, it must throw an error.
      The text of a file is opaque: nothing in it is read as a reference
    - hash: The hash of the artifact, the name, the args, the qualifiers, the content and, when there is one, the source,
      so a change to the file of a `from` or a `ref` changes it. A `by` among the args is left out: who does
      the work is not what the work is, see #{SlytherRole}
}

class SlytherFolderSource {
    Reads a folder the way #{SlytherArtifact} reads a file, so a folder can be the source of a `ref`.

    The files of a folder are every file it holds, each as a path relative to it and what it holds, sorted
    by path. It never walks into node_modules or .git, it obeys every `.gitignore` in the tree, each one
    over the folder that holds it and the ones below, with the last rule that matches deciding, and it
    leaves binary files out: what they hold is not prose, and their bytes would only make the folder look
    changed.

    Its hash covers the path of every file and the hash of what it holds, so adding, removing or renaming a
    file changes it as much as editing one does.

    Its text is everything the folder holds as one text, each file under a heading naming it, for whoever
    cannot read files. A folder that holds more text than the limit must throw an error rather than be cut
    short, since prose that stops halfway is worse than prose that is missing.
}

class SlytherClosureHasher {
    Computes the closure hash of every #{SlytherArtifact} of a list, keyed by `kind:name`.

    The closure hash of an artifact covers its own hash and, transitively, the hash of everything it
    references, so it changes whenever any artifact it depends on changes. A `Parent::child` name is a
    dependency of its parent as well, so a parent changes whenever one of its children changes.

    It is cycle tolerant: mutually referencing artifacts are hashed as a single group, and each member
    of the group mixes its own hash into the group's, so members of a cycle keep distinct closure hashes.

    It also computes the scope hash of every artifact: the same closure but without the children, so it
    covers the artifact and what it references, and stays the same when a child changes. A step is built
    from the scope of its operation and of its kind, so editing a sibling step never rebuilds it.
}

class SlytherArtifactKind {
    A kind of artifact, as defined by an `@artifact` block of a #{ParsedSlytherScript}, contains:

    - artifact: The #{SlytherArtifact} of the kind itself, whose content is the rules of the kind
    - closureHash: The closure hash of the kind, so it changes whenever its rules or any of its operations do
    - scopeHash: The scope hash of the kind, so it changes whenever its rules do but not when an operation does
    - lang: The language of the kind, if its configuration declares one
    - by: The role its configuration declares its work is done by, if any, see #{SlytherRole}
    - packaged: The package the kind was imported from, if the project did not declare it itself
    - params: The shape of every instance of the kind: the params its operations are run with, as
      `name: type` pairs in the order written, after the implicit id
    - operations: Every operation of the kind in the order written, each with its #{SlytherArtifact},
      its closure hash, its scope hash, its params, whether it is deterministic, whether it is built in,
      the role it declares with by, if any, the role that runs its markdown when it is not deterministic,
      and its steps, and each step with its #{SlytherArtifact}, its closure hash, its role and, when it is
      deterministic, its lang. A step is of kind llm or deterministic
    - warnings: The warnings found while grouping the kind, in the order found

    The params of every operation are declared once, by the kind, since what an operation is run with is
    the instance it is run on and never something of its own: the first args of the kind are its params,
    as `name: type` pairs, and they are the shape every instance of it has. Two params are implicit and
    may not be declared: id, the name of the instance, which comes first and is always there, and errors,
    what an update is run to fix, which comes last and only update has. A demanded kind has a third,
    demands, what the artifacts that use an instance ask of it, which follows the id. A param named as an
    arg of an instance is that arg, and any other is its prose, unless it is optional and the instance
    gave no arg for it, see #{Check::SlytherInstanceChecker}.

    An operation declares no types of its own, so a type in its args must throw an error, saying the
    params of a kind are declared on the kind. Written without args, an operation takes every param of
    its kind, which is the whole point of declaring them once. The operations Slyther runs itself are the
    exception and keep their shape, whether they are declared by hand or added as a
    #{SlytherBuiltinOperation}: list takes nothing, since it ranges over every instance rather than one,
    and locate, signature and uses take the id alone. What locate was run with is what finds an instance
    again, so a param that does not say where the artifact is would move it for nothing, see
    #{Check::SlytherInstanceManifest}.

    Written with args, an operation narrows to the params it names, as bare names in the order it wants
    them, which is also how a locate asks for more than the id when the id alone does not say where the
    artifact is. A name that is not a param of the kind, nor id, nor errors on an update, must throw an
    error, and so must a name written twice.

    An instance may only give args the kind declares as params: any other must throw an error, naming the
    instance and what the kind takes, since an arg nothing reads is a typo that would otherwise be lost.

    The args of a kind after its params, and the args of a step, are configuration, and the configuration
    is lang and by: any other must throw an error, and so must lang on an llm step. A step declares no
    params, it receives the params of its operation, and a type as a step arg must throw an error. An
    operation may write a by among the params it narrows to, and no other configuration. A by must name
    one of the roles of #{SlytherRole}, and a kind may not declare a param named by.

    Every step is done by a role. A deterministic step is work that writes, its script; an llm step judges
    in evaluate and writes anywhere else. The by of the step decides, and one of the other line must throw
    an error naming the roles it may take. A script is mechanical whatever it serves, so it is written by
    the scribe unless its own step says otherwise. An llm step takes the by of its operation, then the one
    of its kind, whichever is of the line of its work, since a kind done by an architect says nothing of
    who judges it; else the engineer when it writes and the reviewer when it judges.
    The markdown of an operation that is not deterministic runs in a single session, so it is run by the
    most demanding role among its llm steps.

    The lang of a deterministic step is the one its args declare, else the one of its kind, else the one
    of the #{ParsedSlytherScript}, and when none declares it, it must throw an error.

    The #{ParsedSlytherScript} is only the last word for a kind the project declares itself. The `@lang`
    of whoever imports a package says nothing about the scripts of a kind that package ships: those rules
    were written against a language of their own, and a kind whose rules name `index.ts` would otherwise
    be built as python scripts in a python project, which is a build that works and is wrong. So a kind
    that came from a package inherits nothing from the project, and one that declares no lang, on itself
    or on the step, must throw an error naming the package it came from.

    A kind is built into a folder named after it, with the `::` of its namespace written as a dot, since
    a colon is not a name every file system reads.

    The qualifiers are deterministic, which only an operation may have, and demanded, which only a kind
    may have: any other qualifier, or one written on anything else, must throw an error. A deterministic
    operation runs as a plain script and
    never with an llm, so it must throw an error when it contains an llm step, and when it has no steps its
    content is its only step, a deterministic step named after the operation. Likewise, an operation that
    is not deterministic and has no steps has its content as its only step, an llm step named after the
    operation. An operation whose steps are
    all deterministic but that is not qualified as deterministic is not inferred to be one: it adds a
    warning suggesting the qualifier.

    Its static `of` groups the artifacts of a #{ParsedSlytherScript} into kinds and must throw an
    error when an operation is not declared inside a kind, when a step is not declared inside an
    operation, when an operation that is not deterministic has neither steps nor content, or when a kind has a create
    or update operation but no evaluate operation to verify them with, or when a kind that is not
    composite has operations but no locate operation, or its locate operation is not deterministic or
    does not take the id first. A kind with no operations is allowed: it is declared but not yet defined.

    A kind qualified as demanded has instances nobody declares: one exists because the uses of another
    artifact named it, and what is asked of it is the whole of its spec, see
    #{Check::SlytherInstanceChecker}. So it must define a create operation, since an instance of it could
    never be made otherwise; it must not be composite, since an instance with no code of its own can never
    be asked for a member; its locate must take the id alone, since a demand names an instance and nothing
    else, and there is no declaration to fill anything more from; and every param it declares must be
    optional, for the same reason. Each of these must throw an error. It is declared by hand as any other
    kind: what is demanded is its instances, never the kind itself.

    A kind with an expand operation is composite: an instance of it has no code of its own, only the
    instances its expand emits, as declarations it prints, see #{SlytherExpansion}. So expand must be
    deterministic, the kind must not declare locate, create, update or evaluate, and any other
    operation of it must throw an error. The kinds expand may emit are the ones its prose references,
    and a kind it does not reference is not emitted. A composite kind is not asked for any
    #{SlytherBuiltinOperation}, since there is nothing to list or locate.

    The locate operation is how every other operation finds an artifact of the kind: given its id, and
    whatever else its params ask of the instance, it prints where the artifact is and exits 0, or prints
    nothing and exits 1 when it does not exist. It takes the id first, so an instance is always named to
    it, and takes nothing else unless it names it, since the id alone is what usually says where the
    artifact is: a kind whose locate needs more narrows to the params it needs, and they are filled
    from the instance as the params of create are. An
    artifact may be spread over several files, so it prints one segment per line: a path, for a whole
    file or a whole folder, `path:start-end`, for the lines of a file from start to end, both inclusive
    and starting at 1, or `path:line`, which is the range of that one line, since that is how a line of a
    file is written everywhere else. A folder stands for its structure, what it holds directly, and not for the content
    of its files: a kind that cares about some of them prints them as segments of their own.

    A kind with operations that is not composite also has the #{SlytherBuiltinOperation} it is due, added
    after the ones written, unless it declares an operation of the same name, which replaces the built-in one.
}

class SlytherBuiltinOperation {
    A deterministic operation a kind with operations has without declaring it, so Slyther can generate
    it from the rules of the kind. Each one is a #{SlytherArtifact} of kind operation named `Kind::name`
    with the params, the deterministic qualifier and the content that follow, and no references, so its
    closure hash and scope hash are its own hash. Every kind with operations has list; signature and uses
    only serve to decide whether the artifacts that reference an instance of the kind must be evaluated
    again, so a kind only has them once some artifact references one of its instances, other than
    itself, and a kind nobody references is not asked for them. Two more are asked for them whatever
    references them: a demanded kind, since nothing ever references one of its instances by name, and a
    kind whose rules, operations or steps reference a demanded kind, since an instance of it may ask for
    one and only its uses can say what it asked for. They are:

    - list (): Prints the id of every artifact of the kind that exists, one per line, and exits 0
    - signature (id: string): Prints the public shape of the artifact, one member per line as `key shape`,
      where the key is how code outside reaches the member, its name for what the artifact exports at
      the top level and `Owner.member` for a public member of an exported class or object, and the shape
      is its type or signature, without bodies, private members or comments. Anything whose addition
      breaks the artifacts that use this one, like a required constructor param, is printed as a change
      of an existing line and never as a new line. It reads every segment locate prints. Prints nothing
      and exits 1 when the artifact does not exist, and exits 0 whenever it does
    - uses (id: string): Prints one line per artifact this one depends on, as `kind:id key` for every
      member of it this artifact uses, as `kind:id *` when it uses it as a whole but does not depend on
      the members it may gain, and as `kind:id **` when it depends on every member, including the ones
      it may gain. Every key is written as the signature of that artifact prints it: the name of what it
      imports, and `Owner.member` for every member it reaches through it, never the owner alone. It never
      prints the artifact itself, and it reads every segment locate prints. Prints nothing and exits 1
      when the artifact does not exist, and exits 0 whenever it does
}

class SlytherRole {
    The roles the work of a project is done by, a closed vocabulary so a script means the same wherever it
    is read. Three write, from the lightest work to the most demanding: scribe, for mechanical code like
    the scripts of a kind; engineer, for the code of an instance; architect, for code where design
    matters. Three judge, the same way: checker, for quick checks; reviewer, for a review against the
    requirements; auditor, where a false pass is costly. Engineer and reviewer are the standard role of
    their line.

    Who plays a role is bound by the project with `@role`, see #{SlytherParser}: the provider asked and the
    options handed to it as they are. A role nobody binds is played by the standard role of its line, and
    one whose standard role nobody binds either is played by nobody, which the generator refuses before
    any work is asked for, see #{Project::SlytherProject}. What a cast is recorded as names the role, who
    plays it, the provider and the options in a fixed order, so two casts are recorded alike exactly when
    the same provider is asked the same way.
}

class ParsedSlytherScript {
    Represents a parsed SlytherScript, contains:

    - artifacts: A list of #{SlytherArtifact} that were parsed from the #{SlytherScript}
    - closureHashes: The closure hash of every artifact, keyed by `kind:name`, as computed by #{SlytherClosureHasher}
    - scopeHashes: The scope hash of every artifact, keyed by `kind:name`, as computed by #{SlytherClosureHasher}
    - lang: The language declared by the `@lang` line, if any
    - src: The folder the code of the project lives in, declared by the `@src` line, if any, resolved
      against the script that declared it
    - packages: The package every artifact an `@import` of a package brought in came from, keyed by
      name, so what a package ships is told apart from what the project declares itself
    - roles: Who plays every role the project binds with `@role`, keyed by the role: the provider, the
      options, and where the line was written. It changes no hash
}

class SlytherRunScript {
    A script that runs the project, as defined by a `@run` block of a #{ParsedSlytherScript}. It is always
    built ahead of time into deterministic scripts and never runs prose, contains:

    - artifact: The #{SlytherArtifact} of the script, of kind run, whose content is what the script does
    - name: The name of the script, `default` when the block names none
    - closureHash: The closure hash of the script
    - scopeHash: The scope hash of the script, so it stays the same when one of its steps changes
    - params: The signature it receives when it runs, as the params of an operation
    - lang: The language its args declare, if any
    - steps: Every step in the order written, each with its #{SlytherArtifact}, its closure hash and its lang
    - references: Every artifact the script or its steps reference, so the prose they point to can be read

    The args of a script are its params, which must be types, and its configuration, whose only entry is
    lang. A step is only of kind deterministic, declares no params, and its lang is the one its args
    declare, else the one of its script, else the one of the #{ParsedSlytherScript}, and when none
    declares it, it must throw an error. When a script has no steps, its content is its only step, named
    after the script, as a deterministic operation without steps.

    Its static `of` groups the artifacts of a #{ParsedSlytherScript} into scripts, in the order written.
}

class SlytherExpansion {
    What the expand operation of a composite #{SlytherArtifactKind} printed for an instance of it, read
    into the #{ParsedSlytherScript} that declares the instance. Its static `of` takes the text, the
    instance, the kinds and the parsed script, and returns the script with the emitted artifacts added,
    with their hashes computed as any other, along with the `kind:name` of every one emitted.

    The text is declarations as they are written in a script, each named relative to the instance: an
    `endpoint create` emitted for `crud Users` declares `endpoint:Users::create`, whose scope is the
    instance, so a reference inside it resolves in the instance first, then globally. Every emitted
    artifact references its parent, whether or not its prose writes it, so it receives the prose of the
    parent and is updated when that prose changes.

    It must throw an error, naming the instance and the reason, when the text does not parse with
    #{SlytherParser}, which includes a declaration that takes its prose from a file, when it holds anything but a declaration of an artifact: a directive, a kind, an
    operation or a step; when the kind of a declaration is not one the expand of the parent references,
    or is composite, or has no create operation, since what it emits could never be made; when a
    declaration gives nothing for a param of the create of its kind that is not optional, where id is
    its name, a param named as one of its args is that arg, and any other is its prose; when a reference
    does not resolve; or when a name is already declared, since what a parent emits is never overridden
    by hand.
}

class SlytherParser {
    Reads a #{SlytherScript} and returns a #{ParsedSlytherScript}

    A `@use Name` line opens a namespace: every artifact declared below it is named `Name::artifact`, and
    the namespace itself is declared as an artifact of kind namespace if it was not declared before. A
    reference or an arg type written inside a namespace resolves in that namespace first and falls back
    to the global scope.

    An `@import "path"` line parses another script and adds its artifacts to this one. The path is
    resolved relative to the importing script. An artifact keeps the namespace of the script that
    declares it, so importing never re-homes it, and a script is only read once no matter how many
    times it is imported, so scripts may import each other.

    An `@import "name"` line, written as a bare name rather than a path, imports a package: the
    main.sly of `.slyther/packages/name`, the folder the project installs its packages in, unless the
    parser is given another. Anything with a separator, a dot or a drive is a path, so a package is
    never confused with one, and a package that is not installed must say so naming the package and
    where it was looked for, never as a path that could not be read.

    A package is imported whole and never by the artifact: what an artifact means is what it references,
    so taking one out of a package would leave a reference pointing at nothing, and a type that resolves
    to nothing is left out without a word. Whatever a package pulls in keeps the name of the package
    while it is read, however many relative imports away it was written, so what it declares is known to
    be its.

    An `@lang "name"` line declares the language of the project, the one a deterministic step is written
    in when neither it nor its kind declares one. Writing it twice, in any script, must throw an error.

    An `@role name "provider" (option: "value", ...)` line binds who plays one of the roles of
    #{SlytherRole}: the provider asked, and the options handed to it as they are, each a quoted string, the
    parens optional. Only the project binds roles, in its scripts and whatever they import by path: a
    package says what its work needs with by and leaves who does it to whoever uses it, so a package that
    binds one must throw an error. A name that is not a role, a role bound twice, naming both lines, an
    option that is not a quoted string or an option given twice must throw an error. Switching who plays
    every role is then switching one import, and it changes no hash: only who judges reaches what an
    instance was evaluated with, see #{Check::SlytherInstanceChecker}.

    An `@src "path"` line declares where the code of the project lives, the folder every instance is
    written to and every script, operation and LLM runs from. The path is resolved relative to the script
    that declares it, as an import is, and when no script declares it the project keeps its default.
    Writing it twice, in any script, must throw an error.

    An `@artifact Name (params) (config) { rules }` line declares a kind, as an artifact of the built-in
    kind artifact, and is the only way to make it available: a declaration of a kind that was not declared
    before it must throw an error, and so must writing a kind as a plain `artifact Name` declaration. The
    braces are optional, and the built-in kinds are namespace, operation, llm and deterministic.

    A kind is declared in the namespace it is written in, as anything else is, and the kind a declaration
    opens with is looked up the way a reference is: in that namespace, then in each enclosing one, then
    globally. That is what lets two packages ship a kind of the same name, `Util` and `Util`, and lets
    whoever imports them both name the one it means. A kind that is declared somewhere the declaration
    cannot see it must throw an error naming every namespace that declares it, so the answer is to write
    it qualified or to open its namespace, never to guess.

    Both parens are optional, and they are read as a `@run` reads its own: when there is only one, it holds
    the params when it declares a type and the configuration otherwise, and when there are two, a type in
    the second or a value in the first must throw an error. The params are kept before the configuration,
    so whoever reads the args back tells them apart by what they hold, see #{SlytherArtifactKind}.

    An arg written as a bare name, with no value, is a param of the kind an operation narrows to. It is
    only meaningful where params are declared elsewhere, so a bare name anywhere but the args of an
    operation must throw an error.

    A body may nest blocks, and a nested block is sugar for a `Parent::child` name: an artifact may
    contain operation blocks, an operation may contain llm and deterministic blocks, and no other
    kind contains anything. The nested block's text is left out of its parent's content, and its
    scope is its parent, so a reference inside it resolves in the parent first, then in each
    enclosing scope, then globally. A block may open and close on one line, as `llm input { ... }`.

    Prose is told apart from a nested block by three rules: fenced and inline code is opaque, so
    nothing inside backticks opens, closes or references anything; a line only opens a block if it
    has the shape `kind name (args): qualifiers {`, where the args and the qualifiers are optional,
    and the kind is one its owner may contain, any other line is prose even if it ends in a brace; and
    a line starting with a backslash before such a shape is prose with the backslash removed. A line that opens a block of a known kind where its owner may
    not contain it must throw an error, as must text after the brace that closes a block.

    The content of an artifact is the prose of its body without the indentation its lines share and
    without the blank lines around it, so it reads the same wherever it is written back.

    A `@run name (params) (config) { prose }` line declares a script that runs the project, as an artifact of
    the built-in kind run named `run::name`, inside the implicit namespace run, whatever `@use` is in effect.
    The name is optional and defaults to `default`, and so are both parens: when there is only one, it holds
    the params when it declares a type and the configuration otherwise, and when there are two, a type in
    the second or a value in the first must throw an error. The braces are required, and the body may
    only nest deterministic blocks, its steps. Since run is a kind of its own, declaring `@artifact run`
    must throw an error.

    Any declaration, whether an artifact, a kind, a nested block or a `@run`, may take its prose from a file
    instead of a body, writing `from "path"` or `ref "path"` where the brace would open it. The path is
    relative to the script that declares it. `from` embeds the text of the file as the content, without the
    blank lines around it, so whoever reads the prose reads the file; `ref` only points at it, so whoever
    runs with tools reads the file when it needs it. Since a declaration taken from a file has no body, it
    nests nothing: its children are declared outside, as `Parent::child`. It must throw an error when the
    file cannot be read, or when a brace follows the path.

    A `ref` may point at a folder as well as a file, since pointing at one is no different from pointing at
    the other: the content stays empty and the prose tells whoever runs with tools to read the files the
    folder holds, its hash being the one #{SlytherFolderSource} computes, so adding, removing or editing any
    file in it changes the artifact. The path of a folder is kept with a trailing separator, so whoever reads
    it back knows it is a folder without looking at the disk. A `from` may not point at a folder, which holds
    no text to embed, and must throw an error saying to use `ref`. The path is kept relative to the base the parser
    is given, the folder everything runs from, which is the folder of the script when none is given.

    Declaring the same name twice in the same scope must throw an error.

    It can also read declarations into a script already parsed, under a given artifact, which is what
    #{SlytherExpansion} does with what an expand prints: the declarations are parsed as if written inside
    that artifact, and the hashes of the script are computed again with them. A declaration read this way
    that takes its prose `from` or `ref` a file must throw an error, since what an expand emits has no
    script to be relative to.

    It is created with options: lenient, which records what is wrong in its #{SlytherSourceMap} and keeps
    reading instead of throwing at the first problem, and a reader, which gives what a path holds instead
    of the disk, or nothing to leave it to the disk, so an editor hands it what it has open. Whatever the
    options, it keeps the #{SlytherSourceMap} of the script last parsed, with every declaration, reference
    and path where it was written.

    When lenient, every problem is reported where it was written, on the columns that are wrong when they
    are known, else on the whole line, and reading goes on as if it were not there, in the way that loses
    the least: a lang declared twice keeps the first, a kind unknown or written as a plain declaration is
    declared anyway, a name declared twice keeps the last, a declaration with both a file and a brace
    keeps the brace, a block left unterminated ends with the script, a file that cannot be read leaves the
    prose empty, an import that cannot be read is skipped, a reference or a type that resolves to nothing
    is left out, and a malformed arg is dropped.
}

class SlytherSourceMap {
    Where everything a #{SlytherParser} read was written, and what it found wrong, for an editor to point
    at. Every line and column counts from 0, and the end of a span is the column after its last character.
    It holds:

    - files: Every script read, as an absolute path, in the order read
    - declarations: Every declaration read, in the order read, with its qualified name, its kind, its
      scope, its file, the line it opens on and the last line its body takes, the same line when it has no
      body, and the columns of its kind, or its directive, and of its name on the opening line. A name
      declared twice is listed twice, so both are found
    - references: Every name written that points at a declaration, where it sits: a `#{Name}` in prose
      outside inline code, the type of an arg, or the kind that opens a declaration, with the name as
      written, the scope it resolves in, the declaration it is written in and, once everything is
      declared, the qualified name it resolves to, if any
    - paths: Every path an `@import`, a `from` or a `ref` writes, with the absolute path it points at
    - diagnostics: Everything found wrong, in the order found, each with its message, its severity and
      where it is

    It finds the first declaration of a name, the innermost declaration whose lines hold a line of a file,
    and what is written under a position of a file: the name of a declaration, a reference or a path,
    where a position right after the last character still counts as on it, as a cursor does.
}
